.TH "libnvmf_config_get_discovered_params" 9 "libnvmf_config_get_discovered_params" "October 2026" "libnvme API manual" LINUX
.SH NAME
libnvmf_config_get_discovered_params \- discovered controller parameters.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "int" libnvmf_config_get_discovered_params
.BI "(const struct libnvmf_config *config "  ","
.BI "const char *hostnqn "  ","
.BI "bool is_dc "  ","
.BI "const char *nqn "  ","
.BI "const struct libnvmf_params **params "  ");"
.SH ARGUMENTS
.IP "config" 12
the resolved configuration
.IP "hostnqn" 12
the host NQN that selects the file, or NULL for the top-level
file
.IP "is_dc" 12
true for a discovery controller, false for an I/O controller
.IP "nqn" 12
the NQN of the discovered controller, or NULL
.IP "params" 12
set to the parameters, valid while \fIconfig\fP is alive
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
A controller found through discovery has no section in the
configuration. Its parameters come from one file: the file whose [Host]
section has \fIhostnqn\fP. For a controller found through a discovery
controller of \fIconfig\fP, use \fBlibnvmf_config_conn_get_hostnqn\fP of that
discovery controller's connection.

The parameters are the defaults of that file for a discovery controller
or an I/O controller. If the file also has a section with nqn = \fInqn\fP,
its kxchap-secret and kxchap-ctrl-secret replace the defaults. That
section is a [Discovery Controller] section if \fIis_dc\fP is true, a
[Subsystem] section otherwise. Its controller= lines do not matter. A
section with the well-known discovery NQN never matches.
.SH "RETURN"
0 on success, -EINVAL if \fIconfig\fP or \fIparams\fP is NULL, or -ENOENT
if no file of \fIconfig\fP has \fIhostnqn\fP.
